    "created_at": "2024-07-17T06:20:22Z",
    "updated_at": "2024-07-17T06:20:22Z",
    "body": "Though, can a VideoFrame actually be *not* origin clean?  \r\nIt does throw when the [constructor](https://w3c.github.io/webcodecs/#dom-videoframe-videoframe) is called with a \"dirty\" source, and I don't think any other producer will accept dirty sources[^1], also the VideoFrame interface doesn't have any safe-guard for e.g. the `copyTo` method, which would allow reading of the actual frame's content too. Maybe someone from web-codecs can confirm? (cc @padenot)\r\n\r\nAnd if it is the case that they can't ever be \"origin-dirty\", shall we still include it somehow in that algorithm, maybe at least as a comment?\r\n\r\n[^1]: A `MediaStreamTrackProcessor` might accept producing VideoFrames from a MediaStream captured from an element (video or canvas) which becomes origin-dirty, but the MediaStream is muted in such a case.",
